  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the key uncertainty is whether a standard multimodal (RNA+image) multiprofile pipeline provides a solid baseline and how much the heavier components (ViT-L, transformer aggregation) add over lighter ones. Config_001 (multimodal, multiprofile, scvi_n200, resnet50, avgpool, concat) tests the 'default' multimodal recipe: a learned compact RNA encoder (scvi_n200), a well-established but cheaper image backbone (ResNet-50), simple average-pool aggregation, and concatenation fusion. It establishes a performance reference that all subsequent, more expensive variants (ViT-L, transformer aggregation, weighted fusion) can be compared against. Choosing this relatively inexpensive configuration first preserves GPU budget for later exploitation experiments while still probing the most important design question—whether combining both modalities in a multiprofile setting yields meaningful accuracy. Expected relative cost: low-to-moderate (ResNet-50 and avgpool are significantly cheaper than ViT-L and transformer aggregation; scvi_n200 is a compact 200-dim encoder)."
}

